@[expose] public section

/-!
# The tensor product of two field extensions, one of finite type, is Noetherian Cohen–Macaulay

Proof of Stacks 045M: for `k` a field and `K/k`, `L/k` field extensions one of which is of finite
type as a field extension, the ring `K ⊗[k] L` is Noetherian and Cohen–Macaulay.

Noetherianness is Stacks 045I, which over a field takes the shape
`Algebra.EssFiniteType.isNoetherianRing`: a finitely generated field extension `K/k` makes
`K ⊗[k] L` essentially of finite type over `L`, and `L` is Noetherian, so `K ⊗[k] L` is.

For Cohen–Macaulayness the source argues prime by prime. Write `K` as a finite extension of a
purely transcendental subextension `F = k(t_1, …, t_r) ⊆ K`, which exists because `K/k` has a
finite transcendence basis. Then `F ⊗[k] L → K ⊗[k] L` is a finite free ring map, because `K` is a
finite free `F`-module and `K ⊗[k] L = K ⊗[F] (F ⊗[k] L)`; and `F ⊗[k] L` is a localisation of the
polynomial ring `L[t_1, …, t_r]`. The three steps are carried out here in the generality in which
they are true.

* `F ⊗[k] L` is a localisation of `L[t_1, …, t_r]`
  (`Stacks.CohenMacaulayTensor.exists_isLocalization_mvPolynomial_tensorProduct_adjoin`): the
  subfield generated by a finite algebraically independent family is `k`-isomorphic to the fraction
  field of `k[t_1, …, t_r]` by `AlgebraicIndependent.aevalEquivField`, the ring `L[t_1, …, t_r]
  ⊗_{k[t_1, …, t_r]} Frac(k[t_1, …, t_r])` is a localisation of `L[t_1, …, t_r]` by
  `IsLocalization.tensor`, and the two are identified by the base change `k[t_1, …, t_r] ⊗[k] L =
  L[t_1, …, t_r]`, which is `Algebra.IsPushout.equiv` for `MvPolynomial.instIsPushout`.
* Cohen–Macaulayness of a localisation `G` of `L[t_1, …, t_r]` at each of its primes is Stacks
  00ND, whose conclusion is already stated at an abstract `IsLocalization.AtPrime`, applied
  through `IsLocalization.isLocalization_isLocalization_atPrime_isLocalization`, which exhibits
  `G_𝔭` as a localisation of `L[t_1, …, t_r]` at the contraction of `𝔭`. The base case, that every
  local ring of a field is Cohen–Macaulay, is `isMaximalCohenMacaulay_self_of_isField` of
  `StacksFrontier.RingTheory.CohenMacaulay.Module`.
* Cohen–Macaulayness descends from `G` to any ring `B` finite and flat over it, prime by prime, by
  Stacks 00R5 applied to the flat local homomorphism `G_𝔭 → B_𝔮` for `𝔭` the contraction of `𝔮`.
  Of the two alternatives offered by 00R5 it is the *second*, `dim B_𝔮 ≤ dim G_𝔭`, that is
  available: `B_𝔮` need not be finite over `G_𝔭`, even though `B` is finite over `G`. That
  inequality is an inequality of heights, and it holds because `Spec B → Spec G` is strictly
  monotone for an integral algebra (`Ideal.IsIntegral.comap_lt_comap`).

## Main results

* `Stacks.CohenMacaulayTensor.solution`: the statement of the tag.

## Implementation notes

Cohen–Macaulayness of the ring `R = K ⊗[k] L` is `Module.IsCohenMacaulay R R` (tag 0AAH), the
condition at each prime `𝔭` of `Supp(R) = Spec(R)` that `depth_{𝔭R_𝔭}(R_𝔭) = dim Supp(R_𝔭)`. The
lemma `Stacks.CohenMacaulayTensor.isCohenMacaulay_self_of_forall_isMaximalCohenMacaulay` turns that
into the `Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay` form of tags 00NF, 00ND and 00R5 at each prime: the
localised module `LocalizedModule 𝔭.primeCompl R` is `R_𝔭`-linearly isomorphic to the ring `R_𝔭 =
Localization.AtPrime 𝔭` itself, and `dim Supp(R_𝔭) = dim R_𝔭` by
`Module.supportDim_self_eq_ringKrullDim`. This is the same identification that makes Definition
00NF agree with Definition 00N3 at `M = R`, as in the proof of tag 00NB. `Module.depth` (tag 00LI)
is invariant under a linear equivalence, which is the private lemma
`Stacks.CohenMacaulayTensor.Module.depth_eq_depth_of_linearEquiv`.

The source's "one of `K`, `L` is of finite type" is a disjunction, and the two readings are
interchanged by `Algebra.TensorProduct.comm`. For Noetherianness the transfer is
`isNoetherianRing_of_ringEquiv`. For Cohen–Macaulayness no transfer of
`Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay` along a ring isomorphism is needed either: the descent step is
stated for an arbitrary ring `B` finite flat over `F ⊗[k] L`, so the isomorphism is absorbed into
the `F ⊗[k] L`-algebra structure carried by `B` rather than applied to the conclusion. This is why
`Stacks.CohenMacaulayTensor.isMaximalCohenMacaulay_localizationAtPrime_of_intermediateField` and
`Stacks.CohenMacaulayTensor.isMaximalCohenMacaulay_localizationAtPrime_tensorProduct_of_ringEquiv`
take a ring isomorphism `B ≃+* K ⊗[k] L` as a hypothesis instead of being stated for `K ⊗[k] L`
itself.

## References

* [StacksProject 045M](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/045M)
* [StacksProject 045I](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/045I)
* [StacksProject 00R5](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00R5)
* [StacksProject 00ND](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00ND)
-/


open scoped TensorProduct

namespace Stacks.CohenMacaulayTensor

open Module RingTheory.Sequence

/-- A ring all of whose local rings `R_𝔭` are Cohen–Macaulay in the sense of Definition 00NF,
`depth_{𝔭R_𝔭}(R_𝔭) = dim R_𝔭`, is a Cohen–Macaulay ring in the sense of Definition 0AAH, that is
`R` is a Cohen–Macaulay module over itself. The two readings differ only in bookkeeping:
`LocalizedModule 𝔭.primeCompl R` is `R_𝔭`-linearly isomorphic to `Localization.AtPrime 𝔭`, and
`dim Supp(R_𝔭) = dim R_𝔭`. -/
private theorem isCohenMacaulay_self_of_forall_isMaximalCohenMacaulay (R : Type*) [CommRing R]
    (h : ∀ (p : Ideal R) [p.IsPrime],
      Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay (Localization.AtPrime p) (Localization.AtPrime p)) :
    Module.IsCohenMacaulay R R := by
  intro p _
  have e : LocalizedModule p.asIdeal.primeCompl R ≃ₗ[Localization.AtPrime p.asIdeal]
      Localization.AtPrime p.asIdeal :=
    (IsLocalizedModule.linearEquiv p.asIdeal.primeCompl
      (LocalizedModule.mkLinearMap p.asIdeal.primeCompl R)
      (Algebra.linearMap R (Localization.AtPrime p.asIdeal))).extendScalarsOfIsLocalization
        p.asIdeal.primeCompl (Localization.AtPrime p.asIdeal)
  rw [Module.depth_eq_depth_of_linearEquiv _ e, Module.supportDim_eq_of_equiv e,
    Module.supportDim_self_eq_ringKrullDim]
  exact h p.asIdeal

/-- The `⊥`-depth of a nonzero module is `0`: the only sequence with all terms in `⊥` and of
positive length starts with `0`, which is not a regular element of a nonzero module. -/
private alias depth_bot := Module.depth_bot_eq_zero

/-- A field is a Cohen–Macaulay local ring: both its depth and its dimension are `0`. -/
private alias isMaximalCohenMacaulay_self_of_isField :=
  _root_.isMaximalCohenMacaulay_self_of_isField

/-- Along an integral algebra map the height of a prime is at most the height of its contraction:
`Spec` of an integral algebra is strictly monotone on prime spectra, by
`Ideal.IsIntegral.under_lt_under`. -/
private lemma height_le_height_comap_of_isIntegral {A B : Type*} [CommRing A] [CommRing B]
    [Algebra A B] [Algebra.IsIntegral A B] (q : Ideal B) [q.IsPrime] :
    q.height ≤ (q.comap (algebraMap A B)).height := by
  have hstrict : StrictMono (PrimeSpectrum.comap (algebraMap A B)) := fun _ _ hpp' =>
    Ideal.IsIntegral.under_lt_under hpp'
  have h := Order.height_le_height_apply_of_strictMono _ hstrict (⟨q, ‹q.IsPrime›⟩ :
    PrimeSpectrum B)
  rwa [← PrimeSpectrum.height_eq_orderHeight, ← PrimeSpectrum.height_eq_orderHeight] at h

/-- The height inequality along an integral algebra map, read as the inequality of dimensions
`dim B_𝔮 ≤ dim A_𝔭` between the local rings at a prime and at its contraction. This is the second
alternative in the hypothesis of Stacks 00R5. -/
private lemma ringKrullDim_localizationAtPrime_le_of_isIntegral {A B : Type*} [CommRing A]
    [CommRing B] [Algebra A B] [Algebra.IsIntegral A B] (q : Ideal B) [q.IsPrime] :
    ringKrullDim (Localization.AtPrime q)
      ≤ ringKrullDim (Localization.AtPrime (q.comap (algebraMap A B))) := by
  rw [IsLocalization.AtPrime.ringKrullDim_eq_height q (Localization.AtPrime q),
    IsLocalization.AtPrime.ringKrullDim_eq_height (q.comap (algebraMap A B))
      (Localization.AtPrime (q.comap (algebraMap A B)))]
  exact_mod_cast height_le_height_comap_of_isIntegral q

/-- **Cohen–Macaulayness descends along a finite flat ring map**, prime by prime. This is Stacks
00R5 applied to the flat local homomorphism `R_𝔭 → S_𝔮`, with `𝔭` the contraction of `𝔮`. The
alternative of 00R5 used is the dimension inequality, not finiteness: `S_𝔮` need not be a finite
`R_𝔭`-module even though `S` is a finite `R`-module. -/
@[ega "IV 6.11.9" "Final conclusion"]
private theorem isMaximalCohenMacaulay_localizationAtPrime_of_finite_flat {R S : Type*}
    [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [Algebra R S] [IsNoetherianRing R] [Module.Finite R S]
    [Module.Flat R S]
    (hR : ∀ (p : Ideal R) [p.IsPrime],
      Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay (Localization.AtPrime p) (Localization.AtPrime p))
    (q : Ideal S) [q.IsPrime] :
    Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay (Localization.AtPrime q) (Localization.AtPrime q) := by
  have : IsNoetherianRing S := IsNoetherianRing.of_finite R S
  have : q.LiesOver (q.comap (algebraMap R S)) := ⟨rfl⟩
  let _ := Localization.AtPrime.algebraOfLiesOver (q.comap (algebraMap R S)) q
  exact (Module.isMaximalCohenMacaulay_self_and_ringKrullDim_eq_of_flat (hR _)
    (Or.inr (ringKrullDim_localizationAtPrime_le_of_isIntegral q))).1

/-- **A localisation of a polynomial ring in finitely many variables over a field is Noetherian
and Cohen–Macaulay**, prime by prime. This is Stacks 00ND: its conclusion is stated at an abstract
`IsLocalization.AtPrime`, and `G_𝔭` is a localisation of `L[t_1, …, t_r]` at the contraction of
`𝔭`. -/
@[ega "IV 6.11.9" "special case", ega "IV 6.3.6" "special case"]
private theorem isMaximalCohenMacaulay_localizationAtPrime_of_isLocalization_mvPolynomial
    (L : Type*) [Field L] {σ : Type*} [Finite σ] {G : Type*} [CommRing G]
    [Algebra (MvPolynomial σ L) G] (M : Submonoid (MvPolynomial σ L)) [IsLocalization M G]
    (p : Ideal G) [p.IsPrime] :
    Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay (Localization.AtPrime p) (Localization.AtPrime p) := by
  have hL : ∀ (r : Ideal L) [r.IsPrime],
      Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay (Localization.AtPrime r) (Localization.AtPrime r) :=
    fun _ _ => isMaximalCohenMacaulay_self_of_isField _ (IsArtinianRing.isField_of_isDomain _)
  have : IsLocalization.AtPrime (Localization.AtPrime p)
      (p.comap (algebraMap (MvPolynomial σ L) G)) :=
    IsLocalization.isLocalization_isLocalization_atPrime_isLocalization M
      (Localization.AtPrime p) p
  exact Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay.of_isLocalization_atPrime_mvPolynomial hL
    (p.comap (algebraMap (MvPolynomial σ L) G)) (Localization.AtPrime p)

/-- Cancelling the base change `K ⊗[↥F] (↥F ⊗[k] L) ≃ K ⊗[k] L` restricts along the inclusion of
the right factor to the map `↥F ⊗[k] L → K ⊗[k] L` induced by `↥F → K`. This is the compatibility
that makes `(↥F ⊗[k] L) ⊗[↥F] K ≃ K ⊗[k] L` an isomorphism of `↥F ⊗[k] L`-algebras, the
`↥F ⊗[k] L`-algebra structure on `K ⊗[k] L` being the one given by that induced map. -/
private lemma cancelBaseChange_comp_includeRight {k K : Type*} [Field k] [Field K] [Algebra k K]
    (F : IntermediateField k K) (L : Type*) [Field L] [Algebra k L] :
    ((Algebra.TensorProduct.cancelBaseChange k (↥F) (↥F) K L).toAlgHom.restrictScalars k).comp
        ((Algebra.TensorProduct.includeRight (R := ↥F) (A := K)
          (B := ↥F ⊗[k] L)).restrictScalars k)
      = Algebra.TensorProduct.map F.val (AlgHom.id k L) := by
  ext f <;> simp [Algebra.smul_def]

/-- **Cohen–Macaulayness of `K ⊗[k] L` at each prime, given a subfield over which `K` is
finite.** If `k ⊆ F ⊆ K` with `K` finite over `F`, then `K ⊗[k] L` is finite and flat over
`F ⊗[k] L`, because `K ⊗[k] L = K ⊗[F] (F ⊗[k] L)` is a base change of the finite `F`-module `K`;
so Cohen–Macaulayness descends from `F ⊗[k] L` by Stacks 00R5.

The conclusion is stated for a ring `B` merely isomorphic to `K ⊗[k] L`, so that the caller may
absorb `Algebra.TensorProduct.comm` into the `F ⊗[k] L`-algebra structure on `B` instead of
transporting `Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay` along a ring isomorphism. -/
private theorem isMaximalCohenMacaulay_localizationAtPrime_of_intermediateField
    {k K : Type*} [Field k] [Field K] [Algebra k K] (F : IntermediateField k K)
    [Module.Finite F K] (L : Type*) [Field L] [Algebra k L] [IsNoetherianRing (↥F ⊗[k] L)]
    (hCM : ∀ (p : Ideal (↥F ⊗[k] L)) [p.IsPrime],
      Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay (Localization.AtPrime p) (Localization.AtPrime p))
    {B : Type*} [CommRing B] (e : B ≃+* K ⊗[k] L) (q : Ideal B) [q.IsPrime] :
    Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay (Localization.AtPrime q) (Localization.AtPrime q) := by
  let : Algebra (↥F ⊗[k] L) B :=
    (e.symm.toRingHom.comp
      (Algebra.TensorProduct.map F.val (AlgHom.id k L)).toRingHom).toAlgebra
  let η₁ := Algebra.TensorProduct.comm (↥F) (↥F ⊗[k] L) K
  let η₂ := Algebra.TensorProduct.cancelBaseChange k (↥F) (↥F) K L
  let Θ : ((↥F ⊗[k] L) ⊗[↥F] K) ≃ₐ[↥F ⊗[k] L] B :=
    AlgEquiv.ofRingEquiv (f := (η₁.trans η₂).toRingEquiv.trans e.symm) fun g => by
      change e.symm (η₂ (η₁ (algebraMap (↥F ⊗[k] L) ((↥F ⊗[k] L) ⊗[↥F] K) g))) = _
      rw [show algebraMap (↥F ⊗[k] L) ((↥F ⊗[k] L) ⊗[↥F] K) g = g ⊗ₜ[↥F] 1 from rfl,
        show η₁ (g ⊗ₜ[↥F] (1 : K)) = Algebra.TensorProduct.includeRight g from
          Algebra.TensorProduct.comm_tmul ..]
      exact congrArg e.symm (DFunLike.congr_fun (cancelBaseChange_comp_includeRight F L) g)
  have : Module.Finite (↥F ⊗[k] L) ((↥F ⊗[k] L) ⊗[↥F] K) :=
    Module.Finite.base_change (↥F) (↥F ⊗[k] L) K
  have : Module.Flat (↥F ⊗[k] L) ((↥F ⊗[k] L) ⊗[↥F] K) :=
    Module.Flat.baseChange (↥F) (↥F ⊗[k] L) K
  have : Module.Finite (↥F ⊗[k] L) B := Module.Finite.equiv Θ.toLinearEquiv
  have : Module.Flat (↥F ⊗[k] L) B := Module.Flat.of_linearEquiv Θ.symm.toLinearEquiv
  exact isMaximalCohenMacaulay_localizationAtPrime_of_finite_flat (fun p _ => hCM p) q

section MvPolynomialAlgebra

attribute [local instance] MvPolynomial.algebraMvPolynomial

/-- **`k(t_1, …, t_r) ⊗[k] L` is a localisation of `L[t_1, …, t_r]`**, at the image of the nonzero
polynomials of `k[t_1, …, t_r]`.

Here `k(t_1, …, t_r)` is the subfield `F = k(x_i)` of `K` generated by a finite algebraically
independent family `x : σ → K`, so that `F` is `k`-isomorphic to the fraction field `F₀` of
`k[t_1, …, t_r]` by `AlgebraicIndependent.aevalEquivField`, and
`L[t_1, …, t_r] ⊗_{k[t_1, …, t_r]} F₀` is a localisation of `L[t_1, …, t_r]` by
`IsLocalization.tensor`. The two are identified by the base change
`k[t_1, …, t_r] ⊗[k] L = L[t_1, …, t_r]`, which is `Algebra.IsPushout.equiv`. -/
@[ega "IV 4.2.1.4" "Clause of Remark 4.2.1.4"]
private theorem exists_isLocalization_mvPolynomial_tensorProduct_adjoin
    {k : Type*} [Field k] {K : Type*} [Field K] [Algebra k K] {σ : Type*}
    {x : σ → K} (hx : AlgebraicIndependent k x) (L : Type*) [Field L] [Algebra k L] :
    ∃ (M : Submonoid (MvPolynomial σ L))
      (_ : Algebra (MvPolynomial σ L) (↥(IntermediateField.adjoin k (Set.range x)) ⊗[k] L)),
      IsLocalization M (↥(IntermediateField.adjoin k (Set.range x)) ⊗[k] L) := by
  let ψ : MvPolynomial σ L ≃ₐ[MvPolynomial σ k] MvPolynomial σ k ⊗[k] L :=
    (Algebra.IsPushout.equiv k (MvPolynomial σ k) L (MvPolynomial σ L)).symm
  let h : (MvPolynomial σ L ⊗[MvPolynomial σ k] FractionRing (MvPolynomial σ k))
      ≃+* (↥(IntermediateField.adjoin k (Set.range x)) ⊗[k] L) :=
    (((Algebra.TensorProduct.comm (MvPolynomial σ k) (MvPolynomial σ L)
        (FractionRing (MvPolynomial σ k))).trans
      ((Algebra.TensorProduct.congr AlgEquiv.refl ψ).trans
        (Algebra.TensorProduct.cancelBaseChange k (MvPolynomial σ k) (MvPolynomial σ k)
          (FractionRing (MvPolynomial σ k)) L))).toRingEquiv).trans
      (Algebra.TensorProduct.congr hx.aevalEquivField AlgEquiv.refl).toRingEquiv
  exact ⟨Algebra.algebraMapSubmonoid (MvPolynomial σ L) (nonZeroDivisors (MvPolynomial σ k)), _,
    (IsLocalization.isLocalization_iff_of_ringEquiv _ h).mp inferInstance⟩

/-- **The geometric input of Stacks 045M.** Let `K/k` be a field extension of finite type and
`L/k` any field extension. Then a ring `B` isomorphic to `K ⊗[k] L` is Cohen–Macaulay at each of
its primes.

This is where the source's construction enters: `K` is a finite extension of the subfield
`F = k(t_1, …, t_r)` generated by a transcendence basis — finite because `K/F` is algebraic and
still of finite type — and `F ⊗[k] L` is a localisation of `L[t_1, …, t_r]`, hence Noetherian and
Cohen–Macaulay at each of its primes. -/
@[ega "IV 6.7.1.1"]
private theorem isMaximalCohenMacaulay_localizationAtPrime_tensorProduct_of_ringEquiv
    (k K L : Type*) [Field k] [Field K] [Field L] [Algebra k K] [Algebra k L]
    [Algebra.EssFiniteType k K] {B : Type*} [CommRing B] (e : B ≃+* K ⊗[k] L)
    (q : Ideal B) [q.IsPrime] :
    Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay (Localization.AtPrime q) (Localization.AtPrime q) := by
  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := exists_finset_isTranscendenceBasis k K
  set F := IntermediateField.adjoin k (Set.range (Subtype.val : ↥s → K))
  obtain ⟨M, _, _⟩ := exists_isLocalization_mvPolynomial_tensorProduct_adjoin hs.1 L
  have : Algebra.IsAlgebraic ↥F K := hs.isAlgebraic_field
  have : Algebra.EssFiniteType ↥F K := Algebra.EssFiniteType.of_comp k _ K
  have : Module.Finite ↥F K := Algebra.finite_of_essFiniteType_of_isAlgebraic
  have : IsNoetherianRing (↥F ⊗[k] L) := IsLocalization.isNoetherianRing M _ inferInstance
  exact isMaximalCohenMacaulay_localizationAtPrime_of_intermediateField F L
    (fun p _ => isMaximalCohenMacaulay_localizationAtPrime_of_isLocalization_mvPolynomial L M p)
    e q

end MvPolynomialAlgebra

/-- For field extensions `K/k` and `L/k`, one of them of finite type, the ring `K ⊗[k] L` is
Noetherian and Cohen–Macaulay. -/
public theorem solution (k K L : Type*) [Field k] [Field K] [Field L] [Algebra k K] [Algebra k L]
    (h : Algebra.EssFiniteType k K ∨ Algebra.EssFiniteType k L) :
    IsNoetherianRing (K ⊗[k] L) ∧ Module.IsCohenMacaulay (K ⊗[k] L) (K ⊗[k] L) := by
  rcases h with hK | hL
  · have := hK
    have := Algebra.EssFiniteType.isNoetherianRing L (L ⊗[k] K)
    exact ⟨isNoetherianRing_of_ringEquiv _ (Algebra.TensorProduct.comm k K L).symm.toRingEquiv,
      isCohenMacaulay_self_of_forall_isMaximalCohenMacaulay _ fun q _ =>
        isMaximalCohenMacaulay_localizationAtPrime_tensorProduct_of_ringEquiv k K L
          (RingEquiv.refl (K ⊗[k] L)) q⟩
  · have := hL
    exact ⟨Algebra.EssFiniteType.isNoetherianRing K (K ⊗[k] L),
      isCohenMacaulay_self_of_forall_isMaximalCohenMacaulay _ fun q _ =>
        isMaximalCohenMacaulay_localizationAtPrime_tensorProduct_of_ringEquiv k L K
          (Algebra.TensorProduct.comm k K L).toRingEquiv q⟩

end Stacks.CohenMacaulayTensor


/-!
# The tensor product of two field extensions, one of finite type, is Noetherian Cohen–Macaulay

Let `k` be a field and let `K/k` and `L/k` be field extensions, at least one of them of finite
type as a field extension. Then the ring `K ⊗[k] L` is Noetherian and Cohen–Macaulay.

The mechanism is that a finitely generated field extension is a finite extension of a purely
transcendental one. Say `K` is finite over `k(t_1, …, t_r) ⊆ K`. Then
`k(t_1, …, t_r) ⊗[k] L → K ⊗[k] L` is a finite free ring map, so Cohen–Macaulayness descends
along it by Stacks 00R5 applied at each prime, and `k(t_1, …, t_r) ⊗[k] L` is a localisation of
the polynomial ring `L[t_1, …, t_r]`, hence Cohen–Macaulay by Stacks 00ND. Noetherianness is
Stacks 045I: `K` is the fraction field of a finitely generated `k`-algebra `B ⊆ K`, so `K ⊗[k] L`
is a localisation of the Noetherian ring `B ⊗[k] L`.

## Main results

* `Algebra.TensorProduct.isNoetherianRing_and_isCohenMacaulay_of_essFiniteType`: Stacks 045M.

## Implementation notes

"Field extension of finite type" is `Algebra.EssFiniteType k K`, Mathlib's spelling of finite
generation for a field extension: `IntermediateField.fg_top_iff` reads it as
`(⊤ : IntermediateField k K).FG`, i.e. `K = k(x_1, …, x_n)` for finitely many `x_i ∈ K`. This is
strictly weaker than `Algebra.FiniteType k K`, finite generation as an algebra, which for a field
extension forces algebraicity and would exclude the source's `k(t_1, …, t_r)`; it is the
convention of the neighbouring statements 00PH, 00TU and 030O.

"`K ⊗[k] L` is a Cohen–Macaulay ring" (Definition 00NC: every local ring of it is a
Cohen–Macaulay local ring) is `Module.IsCohenMacaulay R R` for `R = K ⊗[k] L`, the notion of
Stacks 0AAH applied to `R` as a module over itself: for every prime `𝔭` of `R` it asks
`depth_{𝔭R_𝔭}(R_𝔭) = dim Supp(R_𝔭)`, and `Supp(R_𝔭) = Spec(R_𝔭)` for the nonzero ring `R_𝔭`, so
the condition at `𝔭` is exactly `depth_{𝔪}(R_𝔭) = dim(R_𝔭)`, Cohen–Macaulayness of the local
ring `R_𝔭` (Definition 00N8, `Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay R_𝔭 R_𝔭` as in Tags 00R5 and 00ND).
Using the named predicate rather than the quantifier over localisations spelled out in Tag 00ND
keeps the statement citable by the dependents 045N, 05DC and 0BIY, which use Cohen–Macaulayness
of such a tensor product at one of its primes.

The source's "one of them is of finite type" is the disjunction `h`, not a hypothesis on `K`
alone: the two readings are interchanged by `Algebra.TensorProduct.comm`, and the applications
supply either side (Lemma 045N takes `κ(𝔮) ⊗[k] K` with `K/k` the finitely generated one). This
follows Mathlib's `Algebra.TensorProduct.isField_of_isAlgebraic`, which carries the symmetric
hypothesis the same way.

Faithfulness, on the four points a reader should check against the source.
(1) No hypothesis is dead: `h` is what makes the ring Noetherian at all — for
`K = L = k(x_1, x_2, …)` on countably many variables, `K ⊗[k] L` is not Noetherian — and both
field structures are used by the conclusion, which names the `k`-algebra `K ⊗[k] L`; nothing
beyond `k`, `K`, `L` fields and `h` is assumed.
(2) The scalars and the base are the source's: the tensor product is over `k`, and both
conclusions are about the ring `K ⊗[k] L` itself, the depths and dimensions in
`Module.IsCohenMacaulay` being those of its own localisations at its own primes — no base change
and no further extension of `k` intervenes.
(3) The objects the source names are pinned, not merely asserted to exist: the ring is
`TensorProduct k K L` with its canonical commutative ring structure
(`Algebra.TensorProduct.instCommRing`), Noetherianness is `IsNoetherianRing` of that ring, and
Cohen–Macaulayness is the equation of Definition 00N3 at each prime, with the canonical
localisations `Localization.AtPrime`; the conclusion is a conjunction of two properties of a named
ring, not an existence statement.
(4) The instantiation is not degenerate: `k = ℚ`, `K = ℚ(x)`, `L = ℚ(y)` satisfies `h` with an
extension of infinite degree, and `K ⊗[k] L` is then a localisation of `ℚ(y)[x]`, a Noetherian
Cohen–Macaulay ring of dimension `1` — neither a field nor Artinian, so the conclusion is not the
trivial case. The hypothesis has content: dropping `h` admits the non-Noetherian example above.

